Objective: This 21 day email course is designed for the student to gain
a clear understanding of the credit repair process so that they can
approach the client file and create an action plan with certainty. The
content of this course is also designed to help the student effectively
communicate credit information so that as a business owner they can feel
comfortable and confident when speaking about the subject of credit to
leads, clients, and the general public. *The information contained in
this course is not legal advice and should not be taken as such. The
information presented in this lesson is for educational and information
purposes only. If you need legal advice, please seek council*

Bonus Lesson #5:
Disputing Repossessions 

Bruce, How's the dispute process coming along? You have one more
lesson coming your way before the course ends that will sum up the basic
dispute flow, but I wanted to ensure you received all of the bonuses
that I promised. So here is another jam packed value added lesson! The
content you're about to receive is worth more than triple the price you
paid for this course, but I love to under promise and over
deliver...well, in this case under price and over deliver. It's a
quality that has allowed my own credit repair business to exceedingly
meet its quarterly and yearly goals every single year. I hope this is
something that you are applying in your business as well. 
This bonus lesson is all about Auto Repossessions. In it you will learn:

1.   What notices the auto lender must provide to the defaulted
borrower during the auto repossession process, 

2.   The 12 point checklist lenders use as a guide to ensure that
they've covered their tracks when repossessing a vehicle. 

3.   How to properly gather information from your clients to help with
their case when challenging a repo account

4.   How to challenge the furnisher and reporting agencies regarding
an auto repo account.

5.   Factually disputing auto repossessions.

When it comes to Repossessions, the State laws in which the client
resides will be your go to. The following website has great information
about each state's repossession laws
https://educreditsolutions.acemlna.com/lt.php?s=da50c2422345422f9a0dc81917526f09&i=475A492A11A2573
. However, in general,
there are four types of notices your client may receive when their auto
loan is in default and after their car has been repossessed.

1. An Acceleration Notice, informing the borrower that as a result of
the default, the full amount of your loan is due. 

2. A 

**Notice and Opportunity to Cure**, giving the borrower notice of the
default and a time within which you must pay all past due amounts to
avoid repossession.

3. A 

**Post-Repossession Notice**, setting forth the date and time of the
sale.

4. A 

**Post-Sale Notice,**stating any deficiency balance owed after the sale
of the vehicle.

Acceleration Notice

A car loan or lease agreement may contain a provision called an
"Acceleration Clause." This clause allows the lender to demand the full
amount of the outstanding loan if you miss a payment.

For example, if the loan balance is $10,000, and you have missed a
monthly payment of $500, the lender can demand that you pay the $10,000
all at once. If you do not, it can repossess the car.

Some states prohibit a lender from accelerating a loan without notice,
so please check the Repo laws for the state where your client resides.
As a practical matter, most lenders will voluntarily send the borrower a
notice of their intent to accelerate the loan to get them to pay the
past due balance.

Pre-Repossession Notices

In some states, the creditor must provide the borrower with what is
often called an "Opportunity To Cure" Notice before it repossesses the
car. This notice must inform of:

* the amount that is due, and

* the date by which you must pay the amount due.

The lender may combine this notice with an Acceleration Notice. If the
lender has accelerated the contract, the amount due shown on the notice
will be the total outstanding loan balance. Otherwise, the amount due
will be the total of the past due payments.

States that require an Opportunity to Cure often have statutes that
specify:

* how late your payment must be before the loan can be considered to be
in default

* the amount of time you have to cure the default

* the lender's duty to state its intention to repossess the vehicle if
you do not make the payment demanded in the notice, and

* whether certain lenders, such as federal credit unions, are exempt
from the notice requirements.

Be aware some states only require the lender to send this notice once.
If a borrower has previously defaulted on the loan, rehabilitated it,
then default again, the lender does not have to give them an opportunity
to cure the second default.

Post-Repossession Notice

A defaulted borrower may also be entitled to certain types of notices
after the lender repossesses the car.

Most states require the lender to give the borrower an opportunity to
avoid the sale of the car by "redeeming" the loan. The lender is
required to send the borrower a notice outlining what they must do to
redeem the loan, including the amount they will be required to pay to
get the vehicle back.

In most states, the amount to redeem is the full amount of the loan.
However, some states, such as Ohio, a lender is required to accept only
the amount of past due installments to redeem the sale. Check the laws
in your client's state and verify that their lender has complied with
them. 

Notice of Disposition or Notice of Sale

The defaulted borrower is liable for the difference between what their
vehicle sells for and the amount they owe, including any expenses of the
lender in connection with the sale. 

Every state has laws that require the lender to give the defaulted
borrower a Notice of the Sale. That notice must contain:

* a description of the vehicle being sold

* the date of the intended sale, if the car is being sold at a private
sale

* the time, date, and location of the auction if the lender intends to
sell the car at a public auction

* a statement that the borrower may obtain an accounting of any amount
owing on the vehicle,

* contact information so that the borrower can find out more information
about the upcoming sale.

The lender 

**must send this notice at least ten days prior** to the date of the
sale.

Post Sale Notice

After selling the vehicle, the lender must send you a notice that
states:

* the amount it received from the sale

* its expenses in connection with the repossession, and

* the amount of any deficiency you are liable for or the amount of
surplus the lender owes you.

The lender cannot attempt to collect the deficiency until it sends you
this notice. 

If your lender fails to send a required notice, you may not be liable
for any deficiency, and may be able to obtain compensation for damages.

REPOSSESSION CHECKLIST

A creditor is only allowed to sue a defaulted borrower for a deficiency
judgment to collect the remaining amount owed if it followed the proper
procedures for repossession and sale. The borrower may have a legal
defense against a deficiency balance or judgment if the creditor failed
to follow the steps outlined in the checklist below. Also, do not forget
about Statute of Limitations. If a creditor has waited too long before
suing, they are unable to get a judgment for the balance owed whether
they followed these steps or not.

This checklist is used by lenders when proceeding with the steps
necessary to undergo a repossession. You can use this checklist to
gather information from your clients when you're reviewing their credit
report to help you determine your action plan. This checklist can
benefit your dispute efforts by fact checking with the lenders and
requesting evidence of the required documents. You can also use this
information to develop your factual disputing with the bureaus. Let's
take a look...

1. Is the account 10 days or more past due? If not, you have to wait
until it is before you can proceed. 

**Question to Client:**

**Was the account more than 10 days past due before?**

**Request from Furnisher:**

**Provide evidence that the account was more than 10 days past due.**

**2. If the account is 10 days or more past due, have you sent the
NOTICE OF DEFAULT & RIGHT TO CURE? If not, you must send it before you
can proceed.**

**Question to Client:**

**Did you receive both of the following: Notice of Default and Right to
Cure?**

**Request from Furnisher:** Provide evidence that you sent the Notice of
Default and Right to Cure and that these notices were sent in the
appropriate time frame required by (client's state) law. 

**3. Did the NOTICE OF DEFAULT & RIGHT TO CURE give the customer at
least 20 days to cure the default? If not, you have to send the borrower
one that does before you can proceed.**

**4. Did the customer cure the default by paying the account up to date?
If so, and the account is again at least 10 days delinquent, you will
have to send a SECOND NOTICE OF DEFAULT & RIGHT TO CURE, and give the
customer 20 days to cure the default before you can proceed.**

**5. If you sent the SECOND NOTICE OF DEFAULT & RIGHT TO CURE, did it
again give the customer 20 days to cure the default, and did it have the
required additional language? If not, you have to send the borrower one
that does before you can proceed.**

**6. After you repossessed the collateral, did you send the customer the
NOTICE OF OUR PLAN TO SELL PROPERTY?**

**Question to Client:**

**After the automobile was repossessed, did you receive a document
titled "Notice of Our Plan to Sell Property?"**

**Request from Furnisher:**

**Provide me with evidence that you sent and I received the Notice Of
Our Plan To Sell Property within the appropriate time frame required by
(client's state) law.**

**7. If you sent the NOTICE OF OUR PLAN TO SELL PROPERTY, did you give
the customer at least 10 days to redeem the collateral? If not, you have
to send the borrower one that does before you can proceed.**

**8. Was the sale handled in a commercially reasonable manner, e.g.,
advertising for bids, contacting various dealers, etc.?**

**Note:**The law requires that the lender conduct every aspect of the
disposition of the vehicle in a "commercially reasonable" manner, but
that may be interpreted differently in different courts. Commercially
Reasonable usually means - having a motive to keep the bids very low. 

**9. Does the file contain complete information concerning the sale,
include at the very least the nature of the sale (private or auction),
the price obtained for the collateral, the name of the purchaser,
etc.?**

**10. Does the file contain receipts for all expenses incurred in the
repossession, including the repossession fee, repairs, storage, etc.?**

**Request to Furnisher:**

**Provide me with a copy of all the receipts for all expenses incurred
in the repossession, including the repossession fee, repairs, storage,
cost of sell, and any other fees charged under contract as well as the
signed contract with my signature that properly discloses each fee
charged.**

***Can you see where a balance issue may arise in your factual disputing
with the reporting agencies***

**11. If there was a surplus from the sale of the collateral, did you
return the excess funds to the customer?**

**Note:**

**In rare instances, the creditor must pay the borrower if there are
surplus funds after the sale proceeds are applied to the outstanding
contract obligation and related expenses, which is why "Commercially
Reasonable" manner is enforced.**

**12. If you intend to pursue a deficiency balance, did you send the
NOTICE OF SALE AND POSSIBLE DEFICIENCY?**

**Question to the Client:**

**Did you receive the Notice of Sale and Notice of Deficiency Balance?**

**Request to Furnisher:** Prove me with evidence that you sent the
Notice of Sale and a Notice of Deficiency Balance within the appropriate
time frame required by (client's state) law.

When sending in a request for information from the furnisher with a
repo, always start your request list off by stating "To ensure that your
company followed the proper procedure for repossession and sale, I
request that you provide me with all of the following...listing the
items stated above in the checklist...(refer to lesson 20 that explains
how to dispute directly with the furnisher - FCRA 623 & FACTA 312)

A Powerful Fact About Repos...

Did your client sign their auto financing paperwork at the dealership?
Did they finance the automobile through or at the dealership? If so, the
account is probably not a loan, as it is listed on the credit report.
The account type is likely a "retail installment sale" also known as a
"credit sale."

Factually Disputing Repossessions

As with any account, you want to start off analyzing a repo account by
looking for the errors. After verification with the bureaus, if the
account still remains you want to immediately begin disputing with the
furnisher by requesting the items listed in the checklist above. Be
careful when receiving information from the furnisher regarding a repo
account. They are known to send documents that will contain a false 20 -
22 digit tracking number to imply that the proper documents were sent
and they are sure of it, hoping you'd stop there. Don't! Reference the
mail carrier of that tracking number (USPS) and enter the tracking
number to see where it leads. Was it your client's address? An old
address? Many times, it isn't tied to ANY address and it's just a made
up number to deter you from your dispute efforts. If this is the case,
definitely call them out on it. 

With a repo, you can dispute simultaneously with the reporting agencies
and the furnisher. Be sure to check the reports of all of the reporting
agencies that could possibly be reporting the repo, not just Transunion,
Experian and Equifax. Listed below are some examples of what a factual
dispute with a reporting agency might look like. 

Up above, you learned about the Acceleration Notice, that allows the
lender to demand full payment of the loan. This could be grounds for a
Payment Amount Dispute with the Reporting Agencies or Original Creditor
when the account has been accelerated, but the credit report still shows
a payment amount due.

"This alleged account has clearly been accelerated under the laws that
govern this transaction. The payment amount is obsolete and should be
listed as $0. It is a violation of the FCRA to knowingly furnish or
report false information on a consumer report. Immediately delete this
account from my report / Immediately correct this information on my
credit report. This information is misleading and is a
mis-characterization of this alleged account." (choose the wording for
deletion or correction, which ever you're going after). 

In the Little Fact Above, you may have just heard for the first time
that many auto accounts reporting as "loans" are actually a retail
installment sale or credit sale. This could be grounds for an Account
Type Dispute with the Reporting Agencies or Original Creditor when the
account is a result of a credit sale and not a loan.

"You are reporting this account type as a loan when in fact the alleged
agreement is known by its governing laws as a credit sale. There is no
loan agreement in place. No goods or money have been "loaned" to the
alleged debtor. It is a violation of the FCRA to knowingly furnish or
report false information. Immediately delete this item within the time
allowed under the FCRA / Immediately correct this item within the time
allowed under the FCRA." (Choose the wording for deletion or correction,
which ever you're going after)

Bruce, there is sooo much more information that one could learn
about the inaccurate reporting of auto repo accounts on a consumer
report as well many other pieces of data in these accounts that can be
factually challenged with the reporting agencies, original creditors and
collectors, but for the sake of this lesson and the amount of
information you've already consumed and will probably have to re-read a
few more times, I'll end it here. TRUST, you have more than enough
knowledge here to defeat the reporting agencies and creditors by TKO!

